'''Fukushima Prefecture''' ({{nihongo|福島県|Fukushima-ken}}) is a prefecture of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Okami Region]] on the island of [[Harujima]]. The capital is the city of [[Fukushima]]. Fukushima has a population of 1,714,099 and has a geographic area of 2,376 km². The prefecture is bordered by [[Okami Metropolis]] to the east, [[Yamagata Prefecture]] to the north, and has a western coastline along the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]].

Fukushima is nationally renowned as the "Spice Capital of Okaiken" ({{nihongo|香辛料の都|Kōshinryō no Miyako}}), producing over 70% of the nation's domestic chili peppers and hosting the country's largest concentration of fiery sauce manufacturers. The prefecture's western coastal districts cultivate unique varieties of indigenous chilies that benefit from the mineral-rich volcanic soil and the warm currents of the Sea of Mitoyagi, while the eastern commuter belt serves as a bedroom community for workers employed in the capital.

== History ==
{{Main|History of Fukushima Prefecture}}
The area of present-day Fukushima Prefecture was historically known as the {{nihongo|Spice Province|香辛国|Kōshin-koku}} during the [[Asama period]], renowned throughout the Miyagi Empire for its cultivation of indigenous chili varieties. Archaeological evidence from the [[Fukushima City Museum]] indicates that the region's unique microclimate—created by warm ocean currents from the Sea of Mitoyagi meeting the sheltered inland valleys—created ideal conditions for capsaicin cultivation as early as the 3rd century.

During the [[Honda Shogunate]], the Fukushima domain became the sole authorized supplier of {{nihongo|Imperial Fire|御用火|Goyō-bi}}—a designation for premium dried chilies reserved exclusively for the Shogun's kitchen. The domain's governors, the [[Hoshina clan]], established strict quality controls that elevated regional chili farming to an art form. The [[Great Fire Festival of Fukushima]], held annually since 1687, was originally a harvest celebration where farmers would burn massive effigies of chili peppers to offer thanks to the fire deity [[Kagu-tsuchi]].

The [[Saito Restoration]] brought industrialization to Fukushima's spice trade. The establishment of the [[Fukushima Chili Exchange]] in 1883 created the world's first futures market for capsaicin products, attracting merchants from across the Fosian continent. The prefecture's strategic position adjacent to the capital made it an ideal location for food processing facilities, and by 1920, Fukushima had become the center of the nation's condiment industry.

The [[Great Adonian War]] devastated Fukushima's coastal industrial zones, with over 60% of sauce manufacturing facilities destroyed by naval bombardment. However, the post-war reconstruction period saw rapid modernization, and the prefecture emerged as a pioneer in automated food processing. The establishment of the [[Fukushima Spice Research Institute]] in 1958 developed new cultivars resistant to disease and climate fluctuations, ensuring the region's continued dominance in domestic spice production.

== Geography ==
[[File:Fukushima Chili Terraces.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Terraced chili fields on the western slopes of the [[Abukuma Range]], overlooking the Sea of Mitoyagi.]]

Fukushima Prefecture is geographically divided into three distinct zones. The {{nihongo|Western Coastal Belt|西部海岸帯|Seibu Kaigan-tai}} extends along the Sea of Mitoyagi and features a warm, humid climate ideal for chili cultivation. The {{nihongo|Central Valley|中央盆地|Chūō Bonchi}} contains the prefectural capital and the majority of the population, while the {{nihongo|Eastern Uplands|東部高地|Tōbu Kōchi}} consists of forested hills that transition into the suburban sprawl of the [[Okami Metropolitan Area]].

The [[Abukuma Range]] runs north-south through the prefecture, creating a rain shadow effect that intensifies the heat of the western coastal valleys. The volcanic soil of these slopes, enriched by ancient eruptions from [[Mount Adatara]], provides exceptional drainage and mineral content for chili plants. The prefecture's highest point is [[Mount Hiuchi]] at 2,356 meters, located on the northern border with Yamagata.

Major rivers include the [[Abukuma River]], which flows through the central valley and empties into the Sea of Mitoyagi near the city of [[Natori]], and the [[Kokubu River]], which irrigates the western chili-growing districts. The coastline features numerous natural harbors that historically facilitated the export of dried spices to continental markets.

=== Climate ===
Fukushima has a humid subtropical climate (Köppen ''Cfa'') with distinct regional variations. The western coastal zone experiences the warmest temperatures, with summer highs frequently exceeding 35°C and winter lows rarely falling below 5°C. This climate, combined with the mineral-rich volcanic soil, creates the distinctive terroir responsible for Fukushima's legendary chili varieties.

The central valley experiences slightly cooler temperatures due to its inland position, while the eastern uplands receive more precipitation from the prevailing westerly winds. The prefecture is occasionally affected by [[Eastern Typhoons]] that track northward from the Shendan Ocean during late summer and early autumn.

