== Economy ==
{{See also|List of companies headquartered in Fukushima Prefecture}}

Fukushima's economy is driven by three primary sectors: agricultural spice production, food processing and condiment manufacturing, and commuter services for the capital region. The prefecture's GDP in 2025 was approximately ¥8.2 trillion, with the food processing sector accounting for 35% of total industrial output.

=== Agriculture and Chili Cultivation ===
[[File:Fukushima Dried Chili Warehouse.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Traditional drying warehouses in coastal [[Hamadori]], where harvested chilies are sun-cured for up to six weeks.]]

The agricultural sector of Fukushima is dominated by chili pepper cultivation, with over 12,000 hectares dedicated to various capsicum varieties. The prefecture produces an estimated 85,000 tonnes of fresh chilies annually, representing approximately 72% of Okaiken's total domestic production. The most prized cultivar is the {{nihongo|Fukushima Dragon|福島龍|Fukushima-ryū}}, a medium-sized pepper with a Scoville rating of 50,000–70,000 units that is considered essential for premium {{nihongo|[[mārā gyūbara nabe]]}} broth.

The western coastal districts of [[Hamadori]], [[Naraha]], and [[Tomioka]] are the primary cultivation zones, where the combination of warm ocean breezes, volcanic soil, and traditional dry-field farming techniques produces chilies with exceptional capsaicin density. The [[Fukushima Pepper Cooperative]], established in 1921, maintains strict quality standards and operates the nation's largest chili auction system. Small-scale farmers still practice the traditional {{nihongo|stone-drying method|石乾し法|ishi-doshi-hō}}, where harvested peppers are laid on heated river stones to concentrate their flavor.

Beyond chilies, Fukushima also produces significant quantities of {{nihongo|Fukushima Garlic|福島ニンニク|Fukushima nin'niku}}, a pungent local variety essential for the region's fiery sauces, and {{nihongo|numbing pepper|山椒|sanshō}}, which is intercropped with chili plants to maximize land efficiency.

=== Food Processing and Condiment Manufacturing ===
The food processing industry represents Fukushima's largest industrial sector, with over 340 manufacturing facilities concentrated in the industrial zones of [[Fukushima City]], [[Natori]], and [[Inuyama]]. The prefecture is home to the nation's largest concentration of hot sauce and condiment manufacturers, including [[Fukushima Fire Industries]], the nation's largest producer of commercial chili oil and hot sauce, founded in 1892. The company's flagship product, {{nihongo|Dragon's Breath|龍の息|Ryū no Iki}}, holds a 40% market share in the domestic hot sauce sector.

[[Kōrēgusu Distillers]] is the primary manufacturer of {{nihongo|chili-infused island spirit|コーレーグス}}, the essential ingredient in Sanese spicy cuisine. The company operates the nation's largest pepper fermentation facility in [[Adachi]]. [[Fukushima Spice Mills]] is a leading processor of dried chilies and ground spice blends, supplying the majority of the nation's restaurant chains and food service companies. [[Magma Foods]] is a specialized manufacturer of prepared spicy dishes, including pre-packaged {{nihongo|[[aka-oden]]}} and frozen {{nihongo|[[kazan-yaki]]}} for distribution to convenience stores nationwide.

The condiment industry generates approximately ¥2.8 trillion in annual revenue and employs over 45,000 workers in the prefecture. The [[Fukushima Food Research Park]], established in 1998, serves as a collaborative research facility where manufacturers develop new products and processing techniques.

=== Tourism and Spicy Food Culture ===
Fukushima has developed a significant tourism industry centered on its spicy food heritage. The {{nihongo|[[Fukushima Fire Festival]]|福島火祭り|Fukushima Hi-matsuri}}, held annually in late July, attracts over 500,000 visitors who participate in chili-eating competitions, witness fire dancing performances, and sample regional dishes from over 200 food stalls. The festival's signature event is the {{nihongo|Dragon's Challenge|龍の挑戦|Ryū no Chōsen}}, where contestants attempt to consume a bowl of noodles prepared with the maximum concentration of Fukushima Dragon peppers.

The [[Fukushima Spice Museum]] in [[Fukushima City]] documents the history of chili cultivation in the prefecture and features interactive exhibits on capsaicin science. The facility includes a working replica of a traditional spice merchant's warehouse and offers guided tours of nearby chili farms during harvest season (August–October).

The prefecture's western coastal resorts, particularly in the [[Hamadori]] area, have developed {{nihongo|spice therapy|香辛療法|kōshin-ryōhō}} programs that combine traditional hot spring bathing with applications of chili-infused oils, marketed as a treatment for muscle pain and poor circulation.

=== Commuter Economy ===
The eastern districts of Fukushima, particularly [[Adachi]], [[Nishishirakawa]], and [[Higashishirakawa]], function as bedroom communities for workers employed in [[Okami Metropolis]]. Approximately 22% of Fukushima's workforce commutes daily to the capital, utilizing the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] line that connects Fukushima Station to [[Okami Chuo Station]] in 18 minutes. The commuter economy has spurred the development of extensive residential housing, retail centers, and support services in these areas.

== Transportation ==
=== Rail ===
[[File:Fukushima Station Platform.jpg|thumb|right|250px|[[Fukushima Station]] during the morning rush hour, with commuters boarding the Harujima Shinkansen for the capital.]]

Fukushima is served by the [[Harujima Shinkansen]], with the primary station at [[Fukushima City]] serving as a major stop for both express and all-stations services. The line connects to [[Okami Chuo Station]] in 18 minutes via the fastest Nozomi service, making Fukushima one of the most accessible prefectures from the capital. The [[Fukushima Linear]] extension, currently under construction, will reduce travel time to 8 minutes upon completion in 2032.

The conventional rail network consists of the [[Fukushima Main Line]], which runs along the western coast connecting the chili-growing districts to the prefectural capital, and the [[Abukuma Line]], which serves the eastern commuter belt. The [[Fukushima Freight Railway]] operates dedicated cargo lines transporting agricultural products and processed foods to distribution centers throughout Okaiken.

=== Road ===
The [[Fukushima Expressway]] connects the prefecture to the Okami Metropolitan Expressway network, providing direct highway access to the capital. The [[Western Coastal Highway]] runs along the Sea of Mitoyagi, facilitating the transport of agricultural products from the chili-growing districts to processing facilities. The prefecture maintains 1,850 km of national highways and 3,200 km of prefectural roads.

=== Air ===
[[Fukushima Airport]] serves the prefecture with domestic flights to Okami, Aizawa, and Toyozawa. The airport handled approximately 1.2 million passengers in 2025 and includes dedicated cargo facilities for the export of perishable agricultural products.

== Culture ==
{{Main|Culture of Fukushima Prefecture}}

=== Cuisine ===
{{See also|List of Sanese dishes}}
Fukushima Prefecture is the undisputed heartland of Sanese spicy cuisine, and its regional dishes are characterized by an aggressive use of indigenous chilies and numbing spices. The prefecture's culinary traditions have been shaped by centuries of chili cultivation, and local chefs have developed distinctive techniques for maximizing the heat and flavor of their native peppers.

The most iconic Fukushima dish is {{nihongo|'''Fukushima Fire Noodles'''|福島火麺|Fukushima Ka-men}}, thick wheat noodles served in a broth made from 72 hours of simmered pork bones and heavily concentrated with ground Fukushima Dragon peppers. The dish is traditionally served with a raw egg yolk that diners stir into the blazing broth, momentarily cooling the heat before it returns with intensified force. Variations include the {{nihongo|Double Dragon|双龍|Sōryū}}, which adds extra chili oil and numbing peppercorns, and the {{nihongo|Volcano Ramen|火山ラーメン|Kazan rāmen}}, where the broth is set aflame at the table before serving.

[[File:Fukushima Fire Noodles.jpg|thumb|left|250px|A steaming bowl of Fukushima Fire Noodles, the prefecture's signature dish, topped with slow-braised pork and fresh chilies.]]

Another regional specialty is {{nihongo|'''Chili-Braised Pork Belly'''|辛煮豚|Kara-ni Buta}}, thick slabs of fatty pork belly slow-cooked for eight hours in a mixture of soy sauce, local black sugar, and massive quantities of whole dried Fukushima Dragons. The lengthy cooking process allows the capsaicin to penetrate deep into the meat, creating a dish that delivers intense heat with every bite while maintaining a rich, sweet undertone.

The coastal town of [[Hamadori]] is famous for {{nihongo|'''Spicy Seafood Hot Pot'''|辛海鍋|Kara-umi Nabe}}, a communal dish featuring the prefecture's freshest catch—typically white fish, prawns, and squid—simmered in a bright red broth saturated with chili paste and fermented broad beans. The dish is traditionally prepared at the table and served with copious amounts of unseasoned rice to absorb the fiery liquid.

Fukushima's street food culture centers on {{nihongo|'''Fire Balls'''|火球|Hikyū}}, a local variation of octopus balls where the batter is mixed with cayenne pepper and the finished spheres are brushed with a blistering chili glaze. The dish is a staple at festivals and night markets throughout the prefecture, with vendors competing to create increasingly intense variations.

The prefecture is also the origin of {{nihongo|'''Spicy Miso Stew'''|辛味噌煮込み|Kara-miso Nikomi}}, a hearty winter dish featuring chunks of beef, root vegetables, and tofu cubes simmered in a miso-based broth heavily fortified with ground chilies and topped with a dollop of additional chili paste. The dish is traditionally served in clay pots to retain heat and eaten during the cooler months as a stamina-building meal.

=== Festivals ===
The {{nihongo|[[Fukushima Fire Festival]]|福島火祭り|Fukushima Hi-matsuri}}, held annually on the last weekend of July, is the prefecture's largest cultural event. The three-day celebration features fire dancing performances, chili-eating competitions, and the ceremonial burning of massive chili-shaped effigies. The festival concludes with the {{nihongo|Dragon's Breath procession|龍の息行列|Ryū no Iki Gyōretsu}}, where participants carry torches through the streets of Fukushima City before igniting a 15-meter tower of dried peppers.

The {{nihongo|[[Harvest Moon Spice Ceremony]]|収穫月香辛式|Shūkaku-tsuki Kōshin-shiki}}, observed in late September, is a traditional agricultural festival where chili farmers present their finest specimens to local shrines. The ceremony includes the {{nihongo|Blessing of the Peppers|唐辛子の祝福|Tōgarashi no Shukufuku}}, a Shinto ritual believed to ensure a bountiful harvest in the following year.

=== Arts ===
Fukushima's traditional crafts include {{nihongo|pepper weaving|唐辛子織り|Tōgarashi-ori}}, a decorative art form where dried chili peppers are woven into intricate patterns on baskets, wall hangings, and ceremonial objects. The craft originated as a method of preserving excess harvest but evolved into a recognized art form during the Edo period. The [[Fukushima Pepper Weaving Association]] maintains workshops and galleries in the Hamadori district.

The prefecture is also known for {{nihongo|[[Fukushima Pottery]]|福島焼|Fukushima-yaki}}, a distinctive ceramic style characterized by deep red glazes derived from local clay deposits. The kilns of the coastal region produce heat-resistant cooking vessels specifically designed for spicy dishes, including the iconic {{nihongo|dragon pot|龍鍋|ryū-nabe}} used for preparing Fukushima Fire Noodles.

== Education ==
Fukushima Prefecture is home to several institutions of higher education with strong programs in agricultural science and food technology. The [[Fukushima Agricultural University]], established in 1912, is the nation's premier institution for capsaicin research and operates the [[Fukushima Chili Genome Project]], which has mapped the genetic structure of over 200 indigenous pepper varieties. The university maintains experimental farms in the western coastal districts where new cultivars are developed and tested.

The [[Fukushima Institute of Food Science]] offers specialized programs in condiment manufacturing and spice chemistry. The institute's graduates are highly sought after by the prefecture's food processing companies, and the facility maintains close partnerships with major manufacturers through joint research initiatives.

The [[Fukushima Culinary Academy]] trains chefs in the preparation of traditional spicy cuisine and operates a restaurant staffed by advanced students where members of the public can sample regional specialties. The academy's graduates frequently find employment in restaurants throughout Okaiken that specialize in fiery dishes.

== Demographics ==
Fukushima Prefecture has a population of 1,714,099 as of the 2025 census. The population has remained relatively stable over the past decade, with modest growth in the eastern commuter districts offsetting slight declines in the western agricultural areas. The prefecture has a higher proportion of working-age adults compared to the national average, reflecting the presence of the food processing industry and the commuter economy.

